          "topic": "Anti-Defection Law (Tenth Schedule)",
          "question_type": "Statement Elimination",
          "question": "With reference to Anti-Defection Law (Tenth Schedule), consider the following statements:\n1. The Tenth Schedule was inserted by the 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1985 to curb political defections, while the 91st Amendment Act, 2003 omitted the provision allowing a 'split' by one-third of legislature party members and restricted the size of the Council of Ministers to 15% of the strength of the lower house.\n2. The Tenth Schedule was added by the 42nd Amendment Act, 1976 and grants absolute immunity from judicial review to the Speaker's decision.\n\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?",
          "options": [
            "1 only",
            "2 only",
            "Both 1 and 2",
            "Neither 1 nor 2"
          ],
          "correct_index": 0,
          "explanation": "Only Statement 1 is correct.\n• Statement 1 is correct: The Tenth Schedule was inserted by the 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1985 to curb political defections, while the 91st Amendment Act, 2003 omitted the provision allowing a 'split' by one-third of legislature party members and restricted the size of the Council of Ministers to 15% of the strength of the lower house.\n• Statement 2 is incorrect because: In Kihoto Hollohan vs Zachillhu (1992), the Supreme Court held that the Presiding Officer waiting to decide disqualification under the Tenth Schedule functions as a tribunal and their final decision is subject to judicial review on grounds of mala fides or perversity. (The claim that 'The Tenth Schedule was added by the 42nd Amendment Act, 1976 and grants absolute immunity from judicial review to the Speaker's decision.' is factually false).",
