          "subject": "Indian Polity & Constitution",
          "topic": "Governor's Legislative Powers (Articles 200 & 213)",
          "question_type": "Statement Elimination",
          "question": "With reference to Governor's Legislative Powers (Articles 200 & 213), consider the following statements:\n1. Under Article 200, when a State Bill is presented to the Governor, the Governor may give assent, withhold assent, return the Bill (if it is not a Money Bill) for reconsideration, or reserve the Bill for the consideration of the President.\n2. A Governor can return a State Money Bill for reconsideration under Article 200 and promulgate ordinances even when both Houses of the State Legislature are in session.\n\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?",
          "options": [
            "1 only",
            "2 only",
            "Both 1 and 2",
            "Neither 1 nor 2"
          ],
          "correct_index": 0,
          "explanation": "Only Statement 1 is correct.\n• Statement 1 is correct: Under Article 200, when a State Bill is presented to the Governor, the Governor may give assent, withhold assent, return the Bill (if it is not a Money Bill) for reconsideration, or reserve the Bill for the consideration of the President.\n• Statement 2 is incorrect because: Under Article 213, the Governor can promulgate Ordinances only when the State Legislature (or either House in a bicameral legislature) is not in session; such ordinances must be approved by the State Legislature within six weeks of its reassembly. (The claim that 'A Governor can return a State Money Bill for reconsideration under Article 200 and promulgate ordinances even when both Houses of the State Legislature are in session.' is factually false).",
